KLONE
-----

KLone is a web application development framework for embedded systems. It
compiles a web server, its configuration and the site's static and dynamic
(C-scripted) pages into a single executable, kloned.

This branch modernizes KLone 3.1.0 for current toolchains: a reproducible
Linux build against OpenSSL 3, TLS 1.2 or newer, hardened HTTP parsing and
sessions, and automated regression, full-server and fuzz tests. See SECURITY
AND COMPATIBILITY CHANGES below before upgrading an existing deployment.

Original project documentation: http://www.koanlogic.com/klone


QUICK START (LINUX / WSL)
-------------------------

Supported baseline: Linux, Python 3.9 or newer, GNU make, GCC or Clang,
OpenSSL 3 and zlib. On Ubuntu:

    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install build-essential clang libclang-rt-dev libssl-dev \
        zlib1g-dev python3 git patch
    python3 build/build.py --test

On Windows, run the same commands inside WSL from PowerShell in this
repository, for example:

    wsl -d Ubuntu -- python3 build/build.py --test

The daemon is built at .build/release/source/src/kloned/kloned. Build logs
and build-info.json are in .build/release.


BUILD OPTIONS
-------------

    python3 build/build.py [--sanitize] [--test] [--offline] [--incremental]
                           [--jobs N]

    --sanitize     Build with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer
                   into .build/sanitize instead of .build/release.
    --test         Run the regression suite and the full-server tests after
                   building (see TESTING).
    --offline      Use already downloaded dependencies; fail if missing.
    --incremental  Reuse generated build files during development. Omit it
                   for clean, release-quality checks.
    --jobs N       Parallel make jobs (default 2).

How the build works:

* MaKL and LibU are cloned at the exact commits pinned in
  build/dependencies.json. Existing checkouts must match those commits and
  have no tracked modifications (CRLF-only differences are tolerated).
* Sources are copied, with line endings normalized, into
  .build/<mode>/source, and build/makl-modern.patch is applied there. Your
  checkout is never configured or compiled in place, and nothing is
  installed on the system.
* Only one build may use a given .build/<mode> directory at a time; a
  second concurrent build of the same mode is refused.
* build-info.json records the dependency commits, a normalized source
  fingerprint, compiler and OpenSSL versions, and C flags. This reproduces
  the source inputs; identical binaries across different compilers or
  distributions are not guaranteed.
* The build fails rather than silently disabling TLS if OpenSSL 3 is not
  found.


BUILDING AND RUNNING A SITE
---------------------------

KLone embeds your site at build time. build.py produces a daemon with an
empty site; to embed your own pages, import the site directory into the
generated tree and rebuild kloned. For example, with the bundled example
site:

    S=$PWD/.build/release/source
    export MAKL_DIR=$S/makl MAKEFLAGS=-I$S/makl/mk
    cd $S/site
    ../src/tools/klone/klone -c import ../../../../doc/example-site
    make && make depend
    make -C ../src/kloned

Then run the daemon in the foreground with a configuration file:

    ../src/kloned/kloned -F -f /path/to/kloned.conf

-F keeps kloned attached to the terminal and serves requests iteratively;
omit it to run as a forking daemon. -f overrides values in the embedded
configuration. See kloned(8) and kloned.conf(5) in doc/man for all options.
The next clean build discards the imported site, so keep your site sources
outside .build.

HTTPS: the certificate in doc/example-site/etc/kloned.pem is a 1024-bit RSA
key with an MD5 signature that expired in 2006. OpenSSL 3 at the default
security level of common distributions (tested on Ubuntu) refuses it, so
an HTTPS server using it will not start. Generate your own certificate
instead, for example:

    openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:3072 -nodes -keyout key.pem \
        -out cert.pem -days 365 -subj /CN=localhost

and point cert_file and key_file at the new files. Use a certificate from
a trusted CA in production.


TESTING
-------

    python3 build/build.py --test
    python3 build/build.py --sanitize --test

--test runs, in order:

1. The regression suite (95 checks), which compiles selected production
   functions verbatim as C99, with warnings treated as errors, and runs them
   under the sanitizers. LibU and network collaborators are test doubles;
   production MD5 is linked directly.
2. The full-server tests (81 checks), run twice: once in single-process
   mode and once in the normal prefork model. They import a small test-only
   page into the generated build, start kloned on temporary localhost ports
   and check static and HEAD responses, dynamic pages, malformed headers and
   URIs, TLS 1.2 and 1.3 negotiation and rejection of TLS 1.0 and 1.1, cookie
   flags, and memory and client-side session persistence over HTTP and
   HTTPS. Under --sanitize, any AddressSanitizer, LeakSanitizer or UBSan
   report fails the run.

The test-enabled binary contains the test page; never deploy or publish it.
Test certificates and listeners are temporary.

The regression suite also runs without a full build, on Windows or Linux,
with Python 3 and Clang:

    python test/run_regressions.py --sanitize

After a release build, run a bounded libFuzzer test of the real header,
field and utility parsers:

    python3 test/run_fuzz.py --source .build/release/source --runs 10000

Fuzz logs, the corpus and any crash inputs are kept in the build folder.

Continuous integration (.github/workflows/build.yml) runs the build and
tests in release and sanitizer modes, plus the fuzz test, on every push and
pull request, and uploads the build logs.


SECURITY AND COMPATIBILITY CHANGES
----------------------------------

TLS

* TLS 1.2 is the minimum protocol version; TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are rejected.
  TLS compression is disabled. The minimum is fixed in code, not
  configurable.
* OpenSSL 3 is required. Contexts are owned opaque objects accessed through
  public APIs.
* Ephemeral 512-bit RSA and the built-in 1024-bit DH group are gone. Without
  dh_file, OpenSSL selects suitable DH parameters automatically.

Sessions and cookies

* Session IDs carry 128 bits from a cryptographic random source, replacing
  MD5(time, pid, rand()).
* Client-side session MACs default to HMAC-SHA256 (previously SHA-1), use
  checked OpenSSL EVP_MAC calls and compare tags in constant time.
  Client-side session IV and encoded-data lengths are bounds-checked.
  Existing client-side sessions may need to be renewed after upgrading.
  The digest can still be chosen with client.hash_function (kloned.conf(5)).
* Session cookies are sent with Path=/, HttpOnly and SameSite=Lax, plus
  Secure on direct TLS connections. Deletion cookies keep the same scope and
  attributes. Third-party login or embedding flows may need a different
  SameSite policy. TLS terminated by a proxy is not inferred from forwarded
  headers, which are untrusted.
* response_set_cookie() keeps its previous behavior for application cookies.
  The new response_set_cookie_ex() takes explicit RESPONSE_COOKIE_* flags
  (SECURE, HTTPONLY, SAMESITE_LAX, SAMESITE_STRICT, SAMESITE_NONE).

HTTP request handling

* Rejected: duplicate or overflowing Content-Length, any Transfer-Encoding
  (chunked request bodies are not supported), invalid field names, control
  characters in field values, folded headers, unterminated headers, and
  decoded NUL bytes in URI paths.
* Limits: 256 header fields, 8192 bytes per header line, 65536 bytes of
  headers in total.
* The query string is split off before the path is URL-decoded, so an
  escaped '?' (%3F) and a literal '+' in the path are preserved.

Utility APIs

* New capacity-aware u_urlncpy_s(), u_hexncpy_s(), u_htmlncpy_s() and
  u_sqlncpy_s() reject undersized destinations. Internal callers use them;
  the legacy functions remain for compatibility and still require correctly
  sized caller buffers.
* SQL quote encoding now processes the whole input instead of truncating
  after quotes.
* Fixed out-of-bounds reads in URL decoding and substring search, invalid
  hex input handling, undefined behavior in MD5 and path normalization,
  seeking to the end of memory streams, and negative read results being
  treated as large lengths.

Reliability

* The SIGCHLD handler no longer touches the server object, which may be
  unset during startup or shutdown.
* Memory leaks found by LeakSanitizer in kloned shutdown, the page
  translator and the klone tool are fixed.


KNOWN LIMITATIONS
-----------------

* Only Linux (including WSL) is built and tested. Native Windows, *BSD and
  the CyaSSL backend (README.CyaSSL) are not covered by the modern build.
* C++ pages are disabled in the modern build.
* Client-side sessions still use encrypt-then-MAC around AES-CBC. Moving
  that versioned wire format to an AEAD cipher is planned follow-up work.
* Some compiler warnings remain in legacy code and the pinned dependencies.
* Fuzzing is a short smoke test; longer campaigns are still worthwhile.


LEGACY QUICK START
------------------

The original KLone build is kept for reference. It predates OpenSSL 3 and
current compilers, and is not maintained or tested on this branch.

NOTE: KLone needs GNU make in order to be built, which is not the default
      'make' on most *BSD systems (usually you will find it as 'gmake' or
      'gnumake').

1) Configure and build KLone:

    $ ./configure && ${GNUMAKE}

2) Import the example web-application

    [ on Windows edit doc/example-site/etc/kloned.conf and remove syslog
      logging paragraph ]

    $ ${GNUMAKE} depend
    $ cd site && ../src/tools/klone/klone -c import ../doc/example-site
    $ cd .. && ${GNUMAKE}
    $ su
    # ${GNUMAKE} install

3) Launch the KLone executable:

    [ on Windows register the service with "kloned -i" and start it using
      the Services console ]

    $ kloned

4) Start your web browser and go to http://localhost:8080/
